WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:01.630
- Getting into a consulting firm,

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:03.210
like McKinsey, BCG, or Bain,

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.510
is considered a quite
ambitious career goal

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:06.448
for many people.

00:00:06.448 --> 00:00:07.741
So once you're in,

00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:12.300
why the heck would you decide
to quit, to move out again?

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.728
This is exactly the topic
I want to talk about today.

00:00:14.728 --> 00:00:16.560
Welcome to another Coffee Break,

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.139
here on my channel Firm Learning.

00:00:18.139 --> 00:00:19.520
My name is Heinrich.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.000
I'm a former strategy consultant,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.073
and I want to help you
to become successful

00:00:23.073 --> 00:00:25.920
in the first years of your career.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:26.753
So let's talk about
leaving a consulting firm,

00:00:26.753 --> 00:00:29.860
like McKinsey.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.757
And the inspiration for this

00:00:31.757 --> 00:00:34.040
is a video that I saw on the
channel of miss Angie Lu.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.275
She is a rather new YouTuber
who's creating videos

00:00:36.275 --> 00:00:39.020
about consulting-related topics as well.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.740
And here's one video
about why did I choose

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.150
to leave McKinsey?

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.380
You see that it got already 10,000 views,

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.487
which is quite a lot for
a channel of this size.

00:00:47.487 --> 00:00:49.400
So I do like many of the videos.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.210
I encourage you to give her some love

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.430
by subscribing to our channel,

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:53.950
if you enjoy this content as well.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.630
So my plan for today is
to react to this video.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:00:58.490
It's already skip forward to the part

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:00.841
where she starts talking
about leaving McKinsey,

00:01:00.841 --> 00:01:02.112
what her reasons were.

00:01:02.112 --> 00:01:04.270
And I want to look into this with you,

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:06.718
and give you my commentary on her points,

00:01:06.718 --> 00:01:08.340
whether I think they are valid,

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.590
whether I had experiences
similar like this this as well.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.910
So without further ado, let's get into it.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:16.330
- Now, to be honest, why not?

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.210
Why is consulting, or McKinsey,
not the right career for me?

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.000
Well, first of all, I knew that
the lifestyle isn't for me,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.090
the simplest way to find
out, is the future me test.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.510
So if you are wondering the same,

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:28.860
just take a look at the
leadership in your same company.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.140
Do you want to be like that leader?

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.440
Do you admire or enjoy the lifestyle

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.830
that he or she currently has?

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:34.663
As for me, yes,

00:01:34.663 --> 00:01:37.629
the consultant lifestyle was
glamorous to a certain point.

00:01:37.629 --> 00:01:41.650
However, whenever I look at
the partners at McKinsey,

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:42.710
their lifestyle,

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:46.140
I knew deeply that that was not
the life that I had in mind.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.350
- I think that's very valid right?

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:50.220
Thinking about the next best
people in the hierarchy,

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.810
or your leads, your team leads,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.490
the leads of your team leads,

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.980
what does their life look like?

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.712
What is their career looking like?

00:01:56.712 --> 00:01:58.300
And is this something
that you aspire to do?

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.870
And here, I think it's understandable,

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:01.060
if you look at the partners,

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:02.880
how late they work, how busy they are,

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:04.150
how stressed they often are,

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:05.883
and you think for yourself,
and decide for yourself

00:02:05.883 --> 00:02:07.940
, this is not a career for you.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:09.780
The thing that's also fair to recognize,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.520
that even though maybe you
don't want to become a partner

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:13.353
in a consulting firm,

00:02:13.353 --> 00:02:14.867
just starting in coinciding,

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:16.890
and working there for a couple of years,

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.920
still might be a great choice.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.204
The truth probably is that
only a very small minority

00:02:21.204 --> 00:02:23.600
of all the people who joined consulting,

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.650
already decide for themselves that yes,

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:29.040
they want to pursue this career,
until they become partner.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.990
It's rather common for people to say,

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.670
I just want to do this job for
two, or three, or four years

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:34.990
for all the learnings.

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:36.848
And then at some point
I can still reevaluate,

00:02:36.848 --> 00:02:39.204
whether I do actually like it indeed,

00:02:39.204 --> 00:02:40.037
whether I do want to continue,

00:02:40.037 --> 00:02:41.507
or whether I want to move out.

00:02:42.522 --> 00:02:44.460
- I do not want to travel
to different cities

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:45.490
every other day.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:47.630
I do not want to have
to work until 2:00 AM

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:48.463
every single day.

00:02:48.463 --> 00:02:51.600
I also did not want to
be away from my family

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.185
for most of the week.

00:02:53.185 --> 00:02:54.360
- I think this is also very valid.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.370
I made another video

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.026
talking about what the typical work week

00:02:57.026 --> 00:02:58.480
of a consultant looks like.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.390
I will link somewhere above here.

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:03.331
She talks about working until
2:00 AM every single day.

00:03:03.331 --> 00:03:05.153
My experience was not like that,

00:03:05.153 --> 00:03:08.090
that needed to work until
2:00 AM every single day.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.400
But there surely were individual projects,

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.970
where working until 2:00
AM on a regular basis,

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:13.970
wouldn't be rare, right?

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:15.840
So you need to be ready that
things like this happen.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:16.673
And yes,

00:03:16.673 --> 00:03:19.030
you might need to work
significantly after midnight,

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.247
not all the time, but at
least on selected days.

00:03:21.247 --> 00:03:24.223
And sometimes also for several
weeks on certain projects.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.050
- Now second, the constant
pressure and urgency.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:28.883
As you may know,

00:03:28.883 --> 00:03:30.700
consultant projects are typically short,

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:31.940
and very challenging.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.630
And most likely than not,

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.490
the team is always understaffed.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:37.050
That's probably how consulting companies

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.310
make such a high margin per project.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.362
- Yeah.

00:03:40.362 --> 00:03:41.570
So she says a Panasonic
correctly that she believes

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:44.104
the pressure is so high, because
the teams are understaffed.

00:03:44.104 --> 00:03:46.760
Not sure I would a hundred
percent agree with that.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:48.390
So, I mean, we just talked about that.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:49.540
Yes, there is a lot of work,

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.870
lots of things, lots of hours
that you need to put in.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.270
But I don't necessarily agree

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:54.487
that just only if you would have one,

00:03:54.487 --> 00:03:57.018
or two, or three additional
people staffed on the team,

00:03:57.018 --> 00:03:59.831
that this would necessarily
make everyone's life easier.

00:03:59.831 --> 00:04:02.520
So in a, like an MBB environment,

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.580
if we work for a consulting
firm, like BCG or Bain,

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.870
a typical team size would
be two to five people,

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.460
including a project lead.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:12.350
So the team sizes are quite small.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:14.230
I would argue that the main reason

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:16.560
why hours can be sometimes quite long,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.330
is that just during the regular work day,

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:20.950
so from 9:00 AM to 6:00 PM or so,

00:04:20.950 --> 00:04:23.210
you usually spend so
much time with a client,

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.010
conducting interviews, talking to them,

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:26.550
looking at some data that you get,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.080
that you just don't really have time

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.950
to then synthesize all these insights,

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:31.340
to really create your documents,

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.560
do the actual work that you need to do

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.890
to really make progress on the project,

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:38.400
which is why this is often
what you end up doing

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.121
after 6:00 PM in the evening,

00:04:40.121 --> 00:04:42.646
once the clients are not
in the office anymore.

00:04:42.646 --> 00:04:45.970
- So, therefore everything
becomes a priority.

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:46.810
There were so many nights

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.220
where I would remember the
E.M., engagement manager

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:50.590
would say to me that this is a priority,

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.790
that you must finish before tomorrow.

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:55.140
So that's why typically we
work 12 to 15 hour days,

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.020
or even more.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:56.886
And for me as example,

00:04:56.886 --> 00:05:00.288
I actually have experienced panic attacks.

00:05:00.288 --> 00:05:01.178
- Yeah.

00:05:01.178 --> 00:05:02.300
So let's talk about that.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.270
With everything becoming a priority.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.050
So yes, for sure this does happen.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.260
So someone pushing you to, in the evening,

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.570
maybe even at night, complete
something for the next day.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:13.370
I did make the experience, those that,

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:14.500
especially in the first years,

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:15.910
it's like after one or two years,

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:17.560
once you've learned the job a little bit,

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:18.970
like earned your ropes,

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:20.554
you start also learning a little bit,

00:05:20.554 --> 00:05:23.350
this game of pushing
back in the right way,

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:25.220
as a young consultant, as an associate.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.540
I think if you're quite junior
in your first half year,

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:28.600
in your first year,

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.860
you feel like you need to do everything

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:32.080
that you're being asked to do, right?

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.580
Everything just say yes, of course,

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:34.680
I need to do the sense of what,

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.050
even though this means
really working really late.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.340
There are some tips and
tricks, and techniques,

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:41.860
that you just pick up
over time to manage this

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.650
in a bit better way for you.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:45.230
If you're interested in
learn more about that,

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:47.213
just let me know in the comments.

00:05:47.213 --> 00:05:49.303
I might create some additional
videos on this very topic.

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.500
- Where I remember one weekend,

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.810
I was actually flying back to the project,

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:55.032
and as the plane was touching down,

00:05:55.032 --> 00:05:57.130
I just get more and more nervous.,

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:00.230
where I could not even
hear the sound of my Gmail.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:01.063
I kid you not.

00:06:01.063 --> 00:06:03.920
I literally had to delete the Gmail app,

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:05.680
in order for myself to actually calm down.

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:07.439
The third piece is that.

00:06:07.439 --> 00:06:09.356
- So this thing of panic attacks,

00:06:09.356 --> 00:06:11.660
really feeling intense pressure,

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:13.360
really not being able
to handle it anymore.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.380
I mean, yeah surely, you
know, people are different,

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:17.196
people react to stress in a different way.

00:06:17.196 --> 00:06:20.250
I would consider myself probably
quite resistant to stress,

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:23.621
but even myself, I really
had also very, very,

00:06:23.621 --> 00:06:26.560
let's say dark days or dark weeks, right,

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:27.583
in these situations in consulting.

00:06:27.583 --> 00:06:29.819
I mean, I very well remember
being on one project,

00:06:29.819 --> 00:06:32.120
I just felt under so much pressure,

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:33.900
my head was about to explode,

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.243
that's at least how I felt like,

00:06:36.243 --> 00:06:38.690
that I locked myself into
a toilet cabin right,

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:40.540
at one of the client's places.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.480
And I remember having a
meditation app on my phone.

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:44.527
So put on some headphones.

00:06:44.527 --> 00:06:47.710
And I was just trying to
listen to this meditation app

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:48.980
for like 15 minutes or so,

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:50.463
just to calm down a little bit,

00:06:50.463 --> 00:06:53.014
because it just couldn't bear it anymore.

00:06:53.014 --> 00:06:54.400
I mean, the pressure can be intense.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.930
Also here, overtime.

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:57.880
This is an area where you will just

00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:59.893
need to learn some techniques
on how to handle that,

00:06:59.893 --> 00:07:03.393
and how to also dissociate
yourself to some extent,

00:07:03.393 --> 00:07:05.637
to really to work that's
going on the whole day.

00:07:05.637 --> 00:07:06.470
But I mean,

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:10.100
these are things that can be
like a very significant burden

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:12.550
on some people, depending on
how you react to these things.

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:15.600
- Exhausting, from constant
travel and overworking.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:16.970
If you were asking me back then,

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:18.460
what did you do on the weekend?

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:20.511
I will probably tell you, sleep.

00:07:20.511 --> 00:07:21.997
I was sleeping until 11,

00:07:21.997 --> 00:07:23.430
wake up for a quick lunch,

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:25.030
and then keep sleeping or take a nap,

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:27.310
so then I can recover
from the previous week,

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:29.109
and have enough energy for the week after.

00:07:29.109 --> 00:07:32.820
I also remember that I hated,
hated, hated Monday morning.

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:34.905
Yes, I know Mondays are really hard,

00:07:34.905 --> 00:07:38.130
but it's even harder when you
have to wake up at 4:00 AM

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:40.310
every Monday morning to
catch the first flight out.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:41.610
Also you're working until midnight

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.000
pretty much every day of
the week, and because.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.230
- I mean, this is
probably just true, right,

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:48.480
especially for these large
strategy consulting firms,

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.060
BCG, Bain, and so on.

00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.120
I mean, you will just
need to work quite late.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.501
You will often have these
weekends where you just sleep

00:07:54.501 --> 00:07:56.436
because you need to recover from the week.

00:07:56.436 --> 00:07:58.860
And frankly, I think
Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday,

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.100
are probably the hardest days,

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:01.210
at least in the, you know,

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:03.360
pre public health crisis situation

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:04.990
where you just were
traveling all the time,

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:07.570
because Monday morning, indeed,
as the lady just told us,

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:11.070
we wake up often 4:00 AM, 5:00
AM, to get to the airport,

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:12.530
get the first flight out.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:14.950
Then Monday evening, Tuesday
evening, Wednesday evening,

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:16.710
is going to be quite late hours.

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:19.020
Then on Thursday evening,
you might fly back.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.030
This day is already a little bit lighter,

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.910
because then usually at eight, 9:00 PM,

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:25.180
you will be at home and then some emails,

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:26.970
but not that significant work anymore,

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:28.360
at least most of the days.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:29.520
And then Friday,

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:32.720
usually you will be out of the
office at 5:00 PM or 6:00 PM.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:33.940
So nothing too crazy.

00:08:33.940 --> 00:08:35.660
But there are many people
will need some time

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:38.273
to recover on the weekends, indeed.

00:08:39.245 --> 00:08:40.078
- With all of this,

00:08:40.078 --> 00:08:42.366
I actually developed the skill
to be able to fall asleep

00:08:42.366 --> 00:08:45.012
or work, anytime and anywhere.

00:08:45.012 --> 00:08:46.773
Back of the car, easy.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:49.206
On the plane, no problem.

00:08:49.206 --> 00:08:52.280
On the stairs, no challenge at all.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.903
Standing in line, yeah, I got that too.

00:08:54.903 --> 00:08:56.990
- Yeah, it's also true. Right?

00:08:56.990 --> 00:08:58.690
You really just develop this habit

00:08:58.690 --> 00:08:59.995
of just working all the time.

00:08:59.995 --> 00:09:00.828
And to some extent,

00:09:00.828 --> 00:09:03.200
I always wondered whether
this will sort of also like

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:04.420
just the group pressure.

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:05.253
Everyone else was doing it,

00:09:05.253 --> 00:09:06.870
so you felt you needed to do it as well.

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:09.420
But on the other hand, it surely
was also part of, you know,

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:11.150
everything that you
could get done earlier,

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:13.470
just wasn't more time you had
in the evening for yourself

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:14.303
potentially to sleep,

00:09:14.303 --> 00:09:15.540
so this is just what you did.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.810
I remember riding a cab
once, being in a taxi once,

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:20.500
with a couple of other team colleagues,

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:22.570
and then the cab driver was asking us,

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:26.060
"So you guys must be from
this consulting firm, right?"

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:28.140
We just looked at us and like, why?

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:29.360
Like, how do you know, how
do you come up with this?

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.427
And then the cab driver was saying,

00:09:31.427 --> 00:09:32.557
"Well, you guys from this firm,

00:09:32.557 --> 00:09:35.758
"you're the only ones who
always open your laptops

00:09:35.758 --> 00:09:38.640
"in the cab and just continue working."

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.004
- And I really want it
more hands-on experience.

00:09:41.004 --> 00:09:43.160
Yes consulting companies will tell you

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:45.640
that they do implementation work as well,

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:48.650
but typically that's not
true implementation work.

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:50.826
We still stay very high-level,
and very strategic,

00:09:50.826 --> 00:09:53.100
but it's also due to
the nature of our work.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.030
Typically it is unlikely for
you to staff a consultant

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:56.740
for, you know, years to be able

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.077
to finish the implementation project.

00:09:59.077 --> 00:10:01.080
But I really craved that
hands on experience.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:03.120
I really, really, really
wanted it to be able

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:04.190
to build something on my own,

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:05.610
something that I can be proud of.

00:10:05.610 --> 00:10:07.370
And I thought that that
the best way to do that,

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:10.000
is to leave consulting, enjoying startups.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.310
- I mean, to some extent,
I think it's the right.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:12.143
It depends also of course,

00:10:12.143 --> 00:10:13.820
which specific conducting firm your join.

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:15.600
Some are heavy on the strategy side.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.594
Some are more heavy on
the implementation side.

00:10:17.594 --> 00:10:19.830
But also in a strategy consulting firm,

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:22.300
I did work on several
projects which were close,

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:23.230
very close to impact.

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:24.330
I mean, a good example, I think,

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:25.990
is always procurement contracting,

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:26.823
In procurement consulting,

00:10:26.823 --> 00:10:29.230
you really often just work with the client

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:30.240
up until the point

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:32.130
where you really realize certain savings

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:35.340
with your suppliers,
indirect, and direct material.

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:36.980
So depends a bit on the type of project.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:37.813
But of course,

00:10:37.813 --> 00:10:39.222
for sure what you will
not do as a consultant,

00:10:39.222 --> 00:10:41.948
is to take operative responsibility.

00:10:41.948 --> 00:10:45.340
- And last but not least,
to be out of the bubble.

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:46.794
Consultants always have the reputation

00:10:46.794 --> 00:10:48.240
of being the smart one,

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:51.030
but also the arrogant and
the self-centered one.

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:53.344
I'm not saying that everyone
in consulting is like that,

00:10:53.344 --> 00:10:56.080
but I did find myself
for a period of time,

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.267
I was a little bit like that,

00:10:58.267 --> 00:11:00.690
and I felt that I lost
my humility for awhile.

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:02.046
Well, it is because of environment.

00:11:02.046 --> 00:11:04.700
- So here I'd say, I cannot really agree.

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:07.530
I really cannot say that
consultants were self-centered,

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:09.020
or arrogant, or anything like that.

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:12.020
So I met so many, extremely humble people

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:13.310
while working in coinciding.

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:16.520
They were just so kind so
friendly, so empathetic.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:17.815
Surely as in most of these environments,

00:11:17.815 --> 00:11:20.060
they're always are also
people that are not so nice,

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:20.930
or not so great,

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:23.260
people who I do not really
enjoy hanging out with.

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:24.862
But not to say that
consultants in general,

00:11:24.862 --> 00:11:26.490
are so arrogant, and so on.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:28.460
That at least in my humble experience,

00:11:28.460 --> 00:11:29.900
this is not what I saw there.

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:31.820
- Wherein, everyone around us,

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:35.051
are prioritizing us, our
work over everything else.

00:11:35.051 --> 00:11:37.060
Everything that we do becomes a priority.

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:38.700
I mean, think about the
glamorous lifestyle, right?

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:41.160
We literally had a team,
to book cars, and tickets,

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:42.070
and flights for us.

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:43.689
So I was constantly living in this bubble,

00:11:43.689 --> 00:11:46.670
a bubble that has a
reality distortion field,

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:49.470
a bubble that actually separates
us from the real world.

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:51.183
- Here, I agree.

00:11:51.183 --> 00:11:52.570
I mean, these support teams surely exists,

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:54.350
and this is, though, I would argue,

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:55.370
not just all of arrogance,

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:56.680
but just out of necessity.

00:11:56.680 --> 00:12:00.010
So really just to be able
to just reschedule flights

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:00.843
all the time.

00:12:00.843 --> 00:12:02.230
I mean, almost every Friday we took,

00:12:02.230 --> 00:12:04.650
especially on Thursday, got
rescheduled at least once.

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:05.880
That was super common.

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:07.048
Sometimes you would even reschedule it

00:12:07.048 --> 00:12:09.540
after the plane already departed, right?

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.620
I mean then it's probably a
question of personal character,

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:13.643
whether you let this get to you,

00:12:13.643 --> 00:12:14.850
get into your head too much or not.

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:16.664
- It makes us oblivious,
and self-centered,

00:12:16.664 --> 00:12:18.880
and arrogant pricks that we used to hate.

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:21.180
I really need to get out,
before it's too late.

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:22.036
Well, this is my story.

00:12:22.036 --> 00:12:24.772
If you're interested in
exploring a career in consulting,

00:12:24.772 --> 00:12:27.481
I hope my story can give
you a more holistic picture

00:12:27.481 --> 00:12:29.700
of what's a career.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:30.980
- Yeah, I think that
these were the points.

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:32.310
I mean, I provided my commentary.

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:33.570
I would of course be interested,

00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:35.149
what do you think about that,

00:12:35.149 --> 00:12:37.020
whether this matches your
experience in consulting or not?

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:38.550
Also just to be not misunderstood,

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:41.210
my commentary was not
about any specific firm,

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:44.040
or some, necessarily, of
any of my past employers,

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:45.861
but just my general
understanding of what it's like

00:12:45.861 --> 00:12:48.230
to work in consulting, and other firms.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:50.230
While some of these things
really sounded dark,

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:51.350
and maybe not so nice.

00:12:51.350 --> 00:12:52.650
I still believe that consulting

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:54.880
is one of the best career choices

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:57.260
for aspiring young business graduates.

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:59.843
I made plenty of videos
on this topic as well.

00:12:59.843 --> 00:13:01.559
I just do think it is
important, of course,

00:13:01.559 --> 00:13:03.862
to be aware of what you're
getting yourself into,

00:13:03.862 --> 00:13:06.570
because these are realities
that you will face

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:08.100
in many of these consulting firms,

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:09.000
and it would just not be fair

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.310
to not know about these things,

00:13:10.310 --> 00:13:12.530
when you would decide to start
your career in consulting,

00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:14.520
If you took any value
out of this video at all,

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:16.890
please hit the like button
for the YouTube algorithm,

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:19.217
subscribe and all support
on the notifications

00:13:19.217 --> 00:13:21.830
to stay informed on all
